# Copy this file to .env and fill in what you need. .env is gitignored and must
# never be committed; this file is the committed record of what it may contain.
#
# Loaded automatically by utilities.psm1 on import, from ONE place: this
# folder, the package root. Nothing above the package is read.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS REQUIRED. Every command works with no .env at all except the
# two that reach outside: AddPRReview needs a model key, and SendTeams needs a
# webhook. If a command needs a variable, it says so by name and declines -- it
# does not fail halfway.
#
# The agent (tkWeb -Dev) gets these from the shell that starts it, so restart
# it (tkWeb -Stop, then tkWeb -Dev) after you change this file.

# -- AddPRReview --------------------------------------------------------------
# Posts an AI code review to a pull request as inline comments. Get a key at
# console.anthropic.com -- API Keys.
# The key is a secret: here or in the environment, never in a config file this tool writes.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# Fallback name for the same key, read only if ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is unset.
# Set this only if something else on the machine already uses this name.
# CLAUDE_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# -- Teams delivery (communication module) ------------------------------------
# SendTeams and the meeting page's Send button post to a Teams webhook. One
# variable per recipient: TEAMS_WEBHOOK_<RECIPIENT>, the recipient's key in
# capitals -- tkSendTeams manager '...' reads TEAMS_WEBHOOK_MANAGER.
#
# 1. Get the URL in Teams: search "Workflows" -> Workflows -> the template
#      "Send webhook alerts to a chat"     (a person, like your manager)
#      "Send webhook alerts to a channel"  (a team channel)
#    Save it, then copy the URL from the workflow's details page.
#    https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/workflows/send-messages-in-teams-using-incoming-webhooks
# 2. 'manager' is declared already. Any other recipient is declared first, by
#    hand, in config.local.json -- "type" is "channel" even for a person:
#      { "managerWorkflow": { "communication": { "recipients": {
#          "demo_channel": { "type": "channel", "display": "Team channel" } } } } }
# 3. Put the URL below. The AGENT posts, and it reads this file when it starts,
#    so a variable exported in a shell does nothing: restart it (tkWeb -Stop,
#    then tkWeb -Dev) after a change. A send with no agent running fails.
#    Try it unsent first:
#      tkSendTeams manager 'Hello from the Toolkit' -WhatIf
#
# The URL is a secret: here, never in config. A send that Teams accepts is not
# proof it arrived -- check the chat the first time.
# TEAMS_WEBHOOK_MANAGER=https://...
# TEAMS_WEBHOOK_DEMO_CHANNEL=https://...   # declare demo_channel first (step 2)

# Catch-all, used for a declared recipient that has no variable of its own.
# TEAMS_WEBHOOK_URL=https://...

# -- Shell behaviour ----------------------------------------------------------
# Suppress the import-time tier banner. Set by non-interactive callers; not
# normally wanted in a shell you are typing into.
# TOOLKIT_QUIET_STARTUP=1

# Skip registering the nav shortcuts (the cd-to-a-repo aliases) and register
# only the real commands. Useful when another toolkit owns those names.
# PROMPTS_TOOLKIT_NO_NAV_ALIASES=1

# -- Autoload escape hatch ----------------------------------------------------
# The toolkit exports WS_ROOT itself, from wsRoot in config. Set it by hand only
# when the autoload shim was installed as a COPY and cannot find this package as
# a sibling -- point it at the directory CONTAINING your checkout.
# WS_ROOT=C:/ws   # export:example
